706 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 124 0 R/E(Abstract \204 Substance addiction among teenagers is a growing global concern, often leading to severe physical, psychological, and behavioral complications. Early and non-invasive detection methods are essential to prevent long-term damage and improve rehabilitation outcomes. Recent advancements in Artificial Intelligence \(AI\) have enabled the automated identification of subtle oral and salivary biomarkers associated with addictive behaviors. This study proposes a novel Hybrid Multimodal Deep Learning\205Ensemble Fusion \(HMDLEF\) framework for detecting substance addiction through oral health imaging. The model integrates image-based dental features and salivary biomarker data using a multimodal deep learning pipeline to enhance diagnostic precision. Convolutional Neural Networks \(CNNs\) are employed for image feature extraction, while ensemble fusion techniques improve classification robustness and interpretability. By analyzing dental indicators such as enamel erosion, gum recession, and mucosal discoloration, the proposed system aims to support early diagnosis and preventive care. Experimental validation with dental datasets and clinical literature demonstrates the potential of the HMDLEF model as a cost-effective, non-invasive, and reliable AI-assisted screening tool. This research highlights how AI can transform addiction monitoring in dental and public health domains through early intervention and personalized healthcare strategies. )/Pg 38 0 R/K[22]>>
endobj

