712 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(HOW RELIABLE IS AI-GENERATED BACK-END CODE? AN EMPIRICAL STUDY OF CORRECTNESS, SECURITY, AND MAINTAINABILITY IN NODE.JS/EXPRESS APPLICATIONS)/Pg 39 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

713 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 125 0 R/E(Abstract \204 AI coding assistants are now part of everyday development, yet most evidence about how reliable their output is comes from short algorithmic problems rather than the backend work developers actually ship. This paper presents an empirical study of the correctness, security, and maintainability of Node.js/Express code generated by large language models \(LLMs\). We built a suite of 100 realistic tasks, such as login and token handling, file upload, and validated CRUD endpoints, and gave each task to 4 LLMs under three prompt styles: vague, detailed, and detailed with an explicit security instruction. Every solution was run against hidden Jest/Supertest tests, scanned with Semgrep and CodeQL, and measured with ESLint and SonarQube, and tool findings were validated manually on a sample. Across 1,200 generated solutions,  85.0% functional tests,  10.3% 89.7% passed all core contained at least one confirmed vulnerability, and only  were both correct and free of confirmed vulnerabilities. The most frequent weakness classes were [CWE list]. Adding a security instruction changed the vulnerable-solution rate from  10% to 6% , and the models differed in [maintainability finding]. The results suggest that [one-sentence takeaway]. The task suite, hidden tests, and analysis scripts are released to support replication. )/Pg 39 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

