743 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(AN INTEGRATED SATELLITE-GROUND FRAMEWORK FOR SOIL, WATER, AND CROP HEALTH MONITORING USING MULTISPECTRAL REMOTE SENSING )/Pg 40 0 R/K[0]>>
endobj

744 0 obj
<</Type/StructElem/S/P/P 126 0 R/E(Abstract \204 Traditional monitoring of soil condition, water availability and crop health depends on manual field surveys, which are slow, costly and difficult to repeat often enough across a large agricultural area. This paper proposes a simple integrated framework that combines multispectral satellite imagery with Digital Water Level Recorder \(DWLR\) groundwater data to monitor soil, surface water and crop health together. The framework computes five standard spectral indices \204 NDVI, NDMI, NDWI, MNDWI and BSI \204 from satellite bands and combines these values with groundwater level readings collected through the existing DWLR network. By comparing current readings against a historical baseline, the system can highlight early signs of crop stress, changes in soil moisture, and fluctuations in surface and groundwater without repeated site visits. The proposed system relies entirely on satellite missions and government sensor infrastructure that already exist, so no new hardware needs to be deployed. This paper presents the system architecture, the underlying data processing pipeline, and the expected applications and users of such a framework for regional agricultural monitoring. )/Pg 40 0 R/K[1]>>
endobj

